      "note": "Authored directly because the ChatGPT stage cannot run in the cloud session (chatgpt.com blocked, no access to the user's Chrome)."
    },
    "slides": [
      {
        "index": 1,
        "layout": "cover",
        "side": "bottom",
        "kicker": "The 3 ways to compute",
        "headline": "VALUE AT RISK",
        "subheadline": "and what it misses",
        "label": "",
        "body": [],
        "equations": [
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{VaR}_\\alpha = \\inf\\{\\ell : P(L > \\ell) \\le 1-\\alpha\\}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\sigma_p^2 = \\mathbf{w}^\\top \\Sigma\\, \\mathbf{w}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-right"
          },
          {
            "tex": "S_T = S_0\\, e^{(\\mu - \\sigma^2/2)T + \\sigma W_T}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "mid-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{ES}_\\alpha = \\mathbb{E}[L \\mid L \\ge \\mathrm{VaR}_\\alpha]",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "mid-right"
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{VaR}_h = \\mathrm{VaR}_1 \\sqrt{h}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "bottom-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "P(L > \\mathrm{VaR}_\\alpha) = 1-\\alpha",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "bottom-right"
          }
        ],
        "cta": "",
        "footnote": "",
        "illustration": {
          "type": "gbm-band",
          "params": {
            "paths": 120,
            "steps": 220,
            "mu": 0.05,
            "sigma": 0.4,
            "seed": 11
          },
          "labels": [
            "Monte Carlo · 120 GBM paths · σ = 40%"
          ],
          "animate": true,
          "purpose": "Sets the theme: a portfolio's future is a fan of random paths; VaR summarises its bad tail."
        }
      },
      {
        "index": 2,
        "layout": "concept",
        "side": "right",
        "kicker": "",
        "headline": "WHAT VaR SAYS",
        "subheadline": "",
        "label": "One number, one confidence level, one horizon",
        "body": [
          "VaR answers a single question: over the next day, what loss will we not exceed with 99% confidence? It is a quantile of the P&L distribution, nothing more.",
          "A 1-day 99% VaR of $279k means losses should exceed $279k on about 1 day in 100. It says nothing about how large those bad days get."
        ],
        "equations": [
          {
            "tex": "P(L > \\mathrm{VaR}_{\\alpha}) = 1 - \\alpha",
            "role": "hero",
            "slot": null
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\alpha = 99\\%",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "h = 1 \\text{ day}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-right"
          },
          {
            "tex": "L = -\\Delta V",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "mid-right"
          }
        ],
        "cta": "",
        "footnote": "",
        "illustration": {
          "type": "normal-tail",
          "params": {
            "alpha": 0.01
          },
          "labels": [
            "99% VaR",
            "1% tail",
            "99% of days"
          ],
          "animate": true,
          "purpose": "Shows VaR as the cut-off point of the left tail of the P&L distribution."
        }
      },
      {
        "index": 3,
        "layout": "concept",
        "side": "right",
        "kicker": "",
        "headline": "1. PARAMETRIC",
        "subheadline": "",
        "label": "Variance–covariance method",
        "body": [
          "Assume returns are normal. VaR is then volatility scaled by a z-score: portfolio σ comes from the covariance matrix and your position weights.",
          "$10M portfolio, 1.2% daily σ: 2.326 × 1.2% × $10M ≈ $279k. Fast and analytic, but it inherits the normal distribution's thin tails."
        ],
        "equations": [
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{VaR}_{99\\%} = z_{0.99}\\, \\sigma_p\\, V",
            "role": "hero",
            "slot": null
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\sigma_p = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{w}^\\top \\Sigma\\, \\mathbf{w}}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "z_{0.99} = 2.326",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-right"
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\Sigma = \\mathbb{E}[(\\mathbf{r}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu})(\\mathbf{r}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu})^\\top]",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "mid-right"
          }
        ],
        "cta": "",
        "footnote": "",
        "illustration": {
          "type": "cov-matrix",
          "params": {
            "n": 5,
            "seed": 5
          },
          "labels": [],
          "animate": true,
          "purpose": "The covariance matrix assembling cell by cell: the single input the parametric method needs beyond weights."
        }
      },
      {
        "index": 4,
        "layout": "concept",
        "side": "left",
        "kicker": "",
        "headline": "2. HISTORICAL",
        "subheadline": "",
        "label": "Historical simulation",
        "body": [
          "No distribution assumed. Replay the last 500 days of market moves on today's portfolio, sort the P&Ls, and read off the loss at the cut-off.",
          "At 99% with 500 scenarios, VaR is about the 5th-worst loss. Real fat tails are captured, but only the ones in your window: a calm 500 days gives a calm VaR."
        ],
        "equations": [
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{VaR}_{99\\%} \\approx L_{(5)}",
            "role": "hero",
            "slot": null
          },
          {
            "tex": "N = 500",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "500 \\times 1\\% = 5",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-right"
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\hat{F}(x) = \\tfrac{1}{N}\\textstyle\\sum_i \\mathbf{1}\\{r_i \\le x\\}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "mid-left"
          }
        ],
        "cta": "",
        "footnote": "",
        "illustration": {
          "type": "sorted-returns",
          "params": {
            "n": 500,
            "worst": 5,
            "seed": 3
          },
          "labels": [
            "5th worst → VaR",
            "worst day",
            "best day"
          ],
          "animate": true,
          "purpose": "500 historical P&Ls sorted from worst to best, with the five worst highlighted and the VaR cut-off drawn."
        }
      },
      {
        "index": 5,
        "layout": "concept",
        "side": "right",
        "kicker": "",
        "headline": "3. MONTE CARLO",
        "subheadline": "",
        "label": "Simulate the future instead of replaying the past",
        "body": [
          "Choose a model for every risk factor, simulate thousands of scenarios, reprice the portfolio in each, and take the loss quantile of the simulated P&L.",
          "It handles options and other non-linear payoffs that the parametric method misses. The price: compute time, and answers only as good as the model."
        ],
        "equations": [
          {
            "tex": "S_T = S_0\\, e^{(\\mu - \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2})T + \\sigma W_T}",
            "role": "hero",
            "slot": null
          },
          {
            "tex": "dS_t = \\mu S_t\\,dt + \\sigma S_t\\,dW_t",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "W_t - W_s \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,\\, t-s)",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-right"
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{VaR} = -\\hat{q}_{1\\%}(\\Delta V)",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "mid-right"
          }
        ],
        "cta": "",
        "footnote": "",
        "illustration": {
          "type": "mc-paths",
          "params": {
            "paths": 40,
            "steps": 120,
            "mu": 0.06,
            "sigma": 0.3,
            "seed": 21
          },
          "labels": [
            "S₀",
            "simulated paths",
            "at horizon"
          ],
          "animate": true,
          "purpose": "Simulated price paths fan out from today's price; the terminal histogram and its low quantile give the VaR."
        }
      },
      {
        "index": 6,
        "layout": "concept",
        "side": "bottom",
        "kicker": "",
        "headline": "SCALE & BACKTEST",
        "subheadline": "",
        "label": "√time scaling and the Basel traffic light",
        "body": [
          "Regulators asked for 10-day VaR, usually scaled from 1-day: $279k × √10 ≈ $883k. That holds only if daily returns are independent and identically distributed.",
          "Then test the model: over 250 days a 99% VaR should be breached about 2.5 times. Basel counts the exceptions: 0–4 green, 5–9 yellow, 10 or more red."
        ],
        "equations": [
          {
            "tex": "\\mathbb{E}[\\text{exceptions}] = 250 \\times 1\\% = 2.5",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{VaR}_{10d} = \\mathrm{VaR}_{1d}\\sqrt{10}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-right"
          }
        ],
        "cta": "",
        "footnote": "",
        "illustration": {
          "type": "backtest",
          "params": {
            "days": 250,
            "exceptions": 3,
            "seed": 8
          },
          "labels": [
            "green 0–4",
            "yellow 5–9",
            "red 10+"
          ],
          "animate": true,
          "purpose": "A year of daily P&L against the 99% VaR line; three breaches circled, landing in the green zone."
        }
      },
      {
        "index": 7,
        "layout": "closing",
        "side": "bottom",
        "kicker": "",
        "headline": "WHAT VaR MISSES",
        "subheadline": "",
        "label": "",
        "body": [
          "VaR marks where the tail starts, not how deep it goes. Two portfolios can share a VaR while one hides a far worse tail.",
          "Expected Shortfall averages the losses beyond VaR. Basel's FRTB swapped 99% VaR for 97.5% ES: equal-ish under normality (2.338σ vs 2.326σ), but ES grows when tails get fat."
        ],
        "equations": [
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{ES}_\\alpha = \\frac{1}{1-\\alpha}\\int_\\alpha^1 \\mathrm{VaR}_u\\,du",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-left"
          },
          {
            "tex": "\\mathrm{ES}_{97.5\\%} \\approx \\mathrm{VaR}_{99\\%}",
            "role": "decor",
            "slot": "top-right"
          }
        ],
        "cta": "Save this · Follow Risk Hub",
        "footnote": "Figures assume normally distributed daily P&L; real P&L has fatter tails.",
        "illustration": {
          "type": "normal-tail",
          "params": {
            "alpha": 0.025,
            "showES": true
          },
          "labels": [
            "VaR 97.5%",
            "2.5% tail",
            "97.5%",
            "ES"
          ],
          "animate": true,
          "purpose": "Same distribution, with Expected Shortfall marked deeper in the tail than VaR."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
